From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 15:20:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: Add the Lenovo IdeaPad 5G 14Q8X05

Add the Lenovo IdeaPad 5G 14Q8X05 (82KF), a 14" clamshell with the
Snapdragon 8cx Gen 2 5G (SC8180XP). It runs Qualcomm's generic SDM8180
firmware (DSDT QCOMM SDM8180) with Qualcomm's reference input devices,
but its PEP tables, which list the GPIOs and regulator votes Windows
sets for each device, match the Lenovo Flex 5G's for the display, USB,
Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and PCIe. So the tree is sc8180x-lenovo-flex-5g.dts
with these changes.

- The keyboard (ECKB, QTEC0001) is on I2C8 (i2c7). PLST is 0 in the
  dumped DSDT, so _CRS returns the resource at 0x3a and _DSM puts the
  HID descriptor at 0x1. Its GpioInt pin is 384. Windows maps pins
  above the 190 TLMM lines to the interrupts in GIO0's _CRS, one for
  each 64, so 384 is GSIV 556, PDC pin 44 and GPIO 37 in
  sc8180x_pdc_map. The same rule turns the lid's event pin 320 into
  GPIO 121, the pin LID0 reads, and the touchpad's 640 into GPIO 24.
- The touchpad (TCPD, QTEC0002) is at 0x2c or 0x15 on I2C8 with GPIO
  24, picked by CPSD in the SSDT's GNVS region, and its descriptor
  address is HDAD in the same region, which the dump doesn't hold. Both
  addresses are described, with the descriptors the Flex 5G's and the
  Acer Spin 7's DSDTs give those addresses (0x20 and 0x1), as on the
  ThinkPad X13s. The absent one fails to probe.
- I2C8 runs at 400 kHz, as the I2cSerialBus resources say.
- STOR is 0, so it boots from NVMe. The drive sits on PCIe2, the port
  the Dell Inspiron 14 3420 tree enables besides its Wi-Fi port. PEP
  gives PCIe2 CLKREQ# 176 and a power switch on PMC8180C GPIO 11,
  which the firmware leaves on. QPPX gives it PERST# 102, and its wake
  pin 512 maps to GPIO 177. UFS stays off, and so do its two LDOs.
- PCIe3 (5G modem) takes PERST# 178, wake 180 and CLKREQ# 179 as on
  the Flex 5G.
- The panel configuration asks for two eDP lanes at up to 8.1 Gbps,
  so the eDP output has two lanes and link frequencies up to HBR3. The
  panel is a generic edp-panel. GPU0's PEP entry drives GPIO 130
  (panel 3.3 V), GPIO 10 as edp_hot and PMC8180C GPIOs 8 and 10, and
  the backlight section (PMIC PWM at 1 kHz) is the Flex 5G's, so the
  panel supply and the PWM backlight are the Flex 5G's. The Flex 5G's
  VLED_BL_PW switch on PMC8180 GPIO 1 is in no table and is left out.
- There is no touchscreen.
- USB, the WCN3998 Wi-Fi, Bluetooth on UART13 (UR18), the RPMh
  regulators and the remoteprocs follow the Flex 5G.

For the SC8180XP it takes from the Dell Inspiron 14 3420 tree the 3.072
and 3.1488 GHz OPPs of the prime core and the Adreno SMMU interrupts,
which MMU1 in the DSDT lists as GSIV 712 to 719, edge (SPI 680 to 687).
The PMIC RTC reads its offset from the UEFI variable, as on the Dell.

The tree leaves out the USB-C SBU muxes and DisplayPort alt mode, whose
GPIOs are in no table, so only the Type-C orientation inputs 38 and 58
come from the Flex 5G unchecked. It also leaves out Lenovo's embedded
controller at 0x76 on I2C5, the SAR sensors (STH0001, STH0002), the
cameras and audio.

The firmware comes from Lenovo's Windows Update bundle 200.0.3.0. The
ADSP and modem images are signed under Lenovo's own root, the CDSP and
GPU zap shader images under Qualcomm's QMC root. Not booted on the
laptop yet.

Link: https://github.com/aarch64-laptops/build/tree/HEAD/misc/lenovo-ideapad-5g-14q8x05
Signed-off-by: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
